


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: sapp-iwaszko
@title: Digital Encodings of New German Organ Tablatures
@presenter: sapp; iwaszko
@abstract: 

Organ tablatures, especially sources using the New German
style of notation, are very important for the history of
Polish music in the 16th and 17th centuries. They preserve
not only unique repertoire, but also valuable traces of
performance practice. Among the sources of this type, the
richest are the Pelplin Tablatures. This six-volume
collection, preserving almost 900 musical pieces, mainly
vocal music for 2–12 parts, was discovered in the late
1950s and has not yet been transcribed and published, apart
from its facsimile edition.

The size of the repertoire is not the only challenge when
it comes to editing the whole collection. Transcribing the
tablature into common notation does not allow for
preserving the specific features of keyboard notation. On
the other hand, since most of the repertoire is vocal,
using only the original organ notation would not be
particularly useful for performers.

Fortunately, the digital medium allows for multilayer
encoding, preserving the full information while presenting
it in modern notation. For this reason, in 2025 Marcin
Konik, Craig Sapp and Jacek Iwaszko designed a new digital
format for encoding organ tablatures – GOT (German Organ
Tablature). It is based on the TSV format and mimics the
layout of the original tablature notation as closely as
possible. This resemblance makes the transcription process
both quick and accurate. As a result, it was possible to
transcribe the entire repertoire of the Pelplin Tablatures
within just a few months.

The GOT-to-Humdrum converter (got2hum) was developed
together with the format and is available as part of the
humlib library. Additionally, thanks to its TSV-like
syntax, GOT files can be created directly in Google Sheets
or other spreadsheet applications, such as Microsoft Excel.
It was also possible to develop additional validation tools
for Google Sheets, facilitating the transcription and
editing process.

The paper presents the GOT format in detail, as well as the
workflow used to create digital editions of the repertoire
of the Pelplin Tablatures. It also characterises the
musical features of the encoded repertoire and discusses
the possibilities offered by the resulting digital files.

@@END: PRESENTATION



